outputs.with:
**Finding (Correctness / Side Effects — Required):** `rows.sort(...)` mutates `rows` directly, causing unexpected reordering for any consumer holding a reference to the original array. The ternary comparator also fails to return `0` when `a.time === b.time`.

**Scope & Checks:** Reviewed the single line statically; no automated suite ran.

**Correction:**
```ts
const sortedRows = rows.slice().sort((a, b) => a.time - b.time);
```

**Regression Test:**
```ts
import assert from "node:assert/strict";

interface Entry {
  label: string;
  time: number;
}

function sortEntries(rows: readonly Entry[]): Entry[] {
  const sortedRows = rows.slice().sort((a, b) => a.time - b.time);
  return sortedRows;
}

const source: Entry[] = [
  { label: "b", time: 20 },
  { label: "a", time: 10 },
];
const result = sortEntries(source);

assert.deepEqual(result, [
  { label: "a", time: 10 },
  { label: "b", time: 20 },
]);
assert.deepEqual(source, [
  { label: "b", time: 20 },
  { label: "a", time: 10 },
]);
```
